<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In This Region, They Have a Custom Of...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations. Interactive: Contribute to simple discussions on a variety of issues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about Panamanian cultural events and show recognition by answering questions about key details (e.g., "Los Diablos wear masks during the Festival de la Pollera Congo because they represent spirits.").
Can contribute to simple discussions about celebrations and customs in their community by sharing personal experiences and responding to peers' questions (e.g., "What do people typically do during Fiestas Patrias?" "They participate in parades, eat traditional food, and enjoy live music. My family has attended the celebrations for many years, and last year, we saw the parade.").
Can follow a short story about a cultural tradition and recall main details (e.g., "People celebrate with parades.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows 3-4 vivid pictures or very short video clips (10-15 seconds each) of well-known Panamanian celebrations (e.g., Fiestas Patrias parade, Carnival masks, a traditional dance). Students brainstorm words they associate with the images. The teacher guides them to think about "What do people do?" and "What do they use?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation and clarifies the meaning of 3-4 key vocabulary words introduced through the visuals: <em>parade</em>, <em>celebration</em>, <em>mask</em>, <em>dance</em>. Teacher says each word clearly, pointing to the corresponding image, and uses it in a simple sentence. For instance, "This is a **parade**. People **march** in the street." The teacher models word stress: "PA-rade," "cel-e-BRA-tion."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <em>mask</em> something you wear on your hands or on your face?" (On your face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <em>parade</em> usually quiet or loud?" (Loud).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'celebrate' mean to be sad or happy about something?" (Happy).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative (1.5-2 minutes) about two different Panamanian traditions (e.g., Fiestas Patrias and a local harvest festival like Festival de la Mejorana). The narrative describes what people *do* (present simple), what *happened* (past simple), and what they *have done* (present perfect) during these celebrations. It incorporates target vocabulary: <em>tradition</em>, <em>custom</em>, <em>independence</em>, <em>parade</em>, <em>celebration</em>, <em>dance</em>, <em>festival</em>, <em>gather</em>, <em>enjoy</em>, <em>traditional</em>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about Panamanian cultural events and show recognition by answering questions about key details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio for the first time.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the two main traditions or celebrations discussed in the audio?" (Students state the names).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True or False based on the general information.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People in Panama celebrate Fiestas Patrias in December." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"During the festival, people sing and dance." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a third time and match traditions to activities mentioned.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match "Fiestas Patrias" with "participate in parades."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match "Harvest Festival" with "enjoy traditional music."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific grammatical structures (present simple, past simple, present perfect for describing traditions) and target vocabulary in context. Focus on pronunciation of word stress and third-person singular verbs ending in -s, -es, -ies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Listening & Gap-fill):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences from the audio, each containing a blank. Students listen to the audio again (sentence by sentence or short segment by segment) and fill in the missing target vocabulary words (e.g., <em>tradition</em>, <em>custom</em>, <em>parade</em>, <em>celebration</em>, <em>dance</em>, <em>festival</em>). Example: "Panama became independent in 1903. This is a very important ______." (tradition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Listening & Sentence Completion/Categorization):</b> Students listen to specific parts of the audio that highlight grammatical structures. They complete sentences or categorize actions. For example, "People _________ (verb) in the streets during the parades" (march). "My family has ________ (participate) in parades for many years." (participated). The teacher draws attention to the pronunciation of verbs like "decorates" (/s/), "gathers" (/z/), and "wishes" (/ɪz/) as they appear in the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listening & Verification):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of 5-6 key facts about the traditions from the audio. They listen to the audio one last time, checking off the facts they hear and correcting any incorrect information on their list. They then briefly tell their partner one fact they confirmed, using phrases like "I heard that..." or "The audio said...".</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Listen and Share a Local Custom"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher plays a *new*, short (1 minute) audio clip describing a different, perhaps lesser-known, custom or tradition from a specific region in Panama (e.g., "The tradition of 'La Danza del Toro y la Vaca' in certain communities"). The audio uses target vocabulary and grammar at an A2.3 level. Students listen carefully for main ideas and specific details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion and sharing. After listening once or twice, students, in groups of three, discuss what they understood about the custom. They must share at least two specific details they heard (e.g., "People wear masks," "They dance for a long time," "It is a very old tradition"). They should aim to use vocabulary like <em>custom</em>, <em>dance</em>, <em>mask</em>, <em>traditional</em>, and phrases like "In this region, they have a custom of..." or "When they celebrate, they usually..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in recalling listened information, using acquired vocabulary, and demonstrating overall comprehension of the audio. Students practice contributing to simple discussions about celebrations and customs, responding to peers' questions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students in their small groups as they discuss and share details from the "Listen and Share" activity. The teacher can ask a few groups to briefly summarize what they heard for the whole class, checking for understanding of main ideas and specific details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can recognize specific details when listening to explanations (e.g., mentions 2-3 accurate facts from the audio).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows understanding of main ideas and some key vocabulary from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use target vocabulary (e.g., <em>custom</em>, <em>dance</em>, <em>traditional</em>, <em>mask</em>) when sharing information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contribution to discussion is comprehensible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand the main idea of a short audio about traditions." (Yes/Partially/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can identify some new vocabulary words from the audio." (List 2-3 words)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about listening to descriptions of traditions today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging, and what can I do next time to improve my listening for specific details?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What listening strategies worked best for helping students recognize specific details? Were the audio materials and task demands appropriate for A2.3? Some students struggled with the pronunciation of -s/-es/-ies endings; I need to provide more focused drills or a pronunciation guide for common verbs in the next lessons. The gap-fill activity was effective for vocabulary acquisition. I noticed some students relied heavily on the first listen; I need to encourage multiple listens with different foci.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2), we will focus on reading comprehension. Students will read a short text about some of the same Panamanian traditions and customs we discussed today, using the vocabulary we learned. This will reinforce their understanding of the context and target language through a different skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>